 ### [Temporal Sensitivity Pruning](6350-temporal-sensitivity-pruning.md)
-[Temporal Sensitivity Pruning](6350-temporal-sensitivity-pruning.md) is a method for removing low-impact 3D Gaussians by calculating a temporal sensitivity score that approximates the second-order sensitivity to the $L_2$ loss across all training views and timesteps. It sets itself apart from static pruning methods by aggregating gradients over time, thereby capturing the cumulative contribution of each Gaussian to dynamic scene reconstruction and stabilizing pruning in deformable settings.
+Temporal Sensitivity Pruning (TSP) is a method for removing low-impact 3D Gaussians by calculating a temporal sensitivity score that approximates the second-order sensitivity to the $L_2$ loss across all training views and timesteps. It sets itself apart from static pruning methods by aggregating gradients over time, thereby capturing the cumulative contribution of each Gaussian to dynamic scene reconstruction and stabilizing pruning in deformable settings.
 
 ### [Opacity Split for Cloning](9694-opacity-split-for-cloning.md)
-[Opacity Split for Cloning](9694-opacity-split-for-cloning.md) is a method used during the densification phase of 3D Gaussian Splatting to clone a parent Gaussian into $N$ copies while minimizing the difference between the rasterization outcomes before and after cloning. It solves the problem where standard cloning causes the parent and child to collectively exert greater transmittance impact than the parent alone, biasing the alpha blending process. It sets itself apart from standard 3DGS cloning by explicitly deriving opacity and covariance adjustments to preserve rendering intensity, rather than simply inheriting parent parameters.
